    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\nMagellan Discoverer Is the First Hybrid-Electric Polar Cruise Ship Built in South America\n7 photos\nPhoto: Antarctica21\nThe newest ship ready to join the fleet of Antarctica21, a leading name in boutique, small-ship expeditions to the White Continent, is a pioneer for shipbuilding in the Americas. Magellan Discoverer marks a major milestone for the biggest shipyard in Chile, as South America’s first hybrid-electric expedition ship.\nAsenav (Astilleros y Servicios Navales S.A.) announced the beginning of sea trials for Magellan Discoverer, an expedition cruise ship unlike any other built in the region. Cruise operator Antarctica21 commissioned it in 2023, based on the successful platform of Megallan Explorer, which was also built by Asenav and delivered in 2019.\nThe new Magellan Discoverer is, first of all, a rugged polar cruise ship with a reinforced hull and the ability to break through ice that’s 80 cm (2.6 feet) thick. Complete with a Polar Class PC6 rating and an IMO Tier III certification (International Maritime Organization’s classification for maximum emission levels permitted), this new-generation cruise ship is meant to provide safe, efficient travel to the White Continent, while also protecting wildlife and the environment.\nAccording to Antarctica21, every system on board was specifically engineered to reduce environmental impact, so it’s not just about the hybrid-electric propulsion. The advanced anti-fouling system, for instance, prevents marine organism from getting attached to the hull, which in turn helps reduce drag, but without the toxic biocides used previously in cruising.\nThe ballast water management system uses treated seawater to maintain the stability of the ship, in line with current environmental regulations to prevent ecological disruption. The onboard waste management system also meets stringent IMO requirements. The integrated heat recovery system captures waste heat from the engine exhaust gases and then uses its onboard hotel systems, which reduces reliance on fuel-fired boilers to a great extent.\nSome of these advanced features, including the reverse osmosis water system that converts seawater into freshwater, are also involved in keeping this long-range expedition ship autonomous during longer periods of time. As for the hybrid-electric propulsion system, it features two MAN main engines, the ABB hybrid DC grid (a modular platform that combines generator power with battery storage), and ABB Azipod propulsion.\nThe Corvus Energy Battery Storage System allows zero-emission operations in sensitive environments, as well as lower noise levels, and overall more flexible energy management. The Azipods ensure so-called 360-degree propulsion, which translates to better maneuverability, reduced underwater noise, and a lower impact on wildlife.\nThe 94-meter (308 feet) ship built to accommodate up to 96 passengers is set to enter service with Antarctica21 on December 15, this year. For the operator’s signature fly-cruise programs, where passengers avoid the challenging Drake Passage by flying straight to King George Island, where they embark on the ship, Magellan Discoverer will be limited to 88 guests.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\npolar cruise ship expedition cruise ship hybrid polar cruise ship Magellan Discoverer Antarctica21 cruise ship\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT Audi R8 vs. Nissan GT-R Drag Race Is Over in 8 Seconds – Video",
